@NODE Introduction "Introduction"
@TITLE "Introduction"


AmiPlexAmp is a native MUI 3.8 client for Plex Media Server that lets
you browse and play your audio library directly from an AmigaOS 3
machine. It connects to your Plex server over the local network,
decodes MP3 streams with mpega.library and FLAC streams with dr_flac,
and outputs through either AHI or directly through the SAGA HQ audio
channel on Apollo Vampire boards.

The application is a native AmigaOS 3.0+ executable targeting
m68k. It runs on stock 68030 systems and scales up to the Apollo
Vampire 68080 with SAGA-accelerated audio output.

This documentation covers installation, usage, configuration and
troubleshooting. The corresponding Aminet ReadMe in this same archive
gives a shorter overview.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Features "Features"
@TITLE "Features"


  - Plex authentication via the plex.tv/link PIN flow (no password
    ever typed into AmigaOS). The auth token is persisted in
    PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.config so you only do this once.

  - Automatic discovery of Plex servers on your account, with a
    picker when more than one is reachable. Same for music
    libraries on the selected server. See
    @{"Servers and libraries" LINK Servers}.

  - Hierarchical library browsing: alphabetical filter, artists,
    albums, tracks. Cover art via datatypes on AGA, via
    cybergraphics.library WritePixelArray for 24-bit RTG.

  - @{"Library search" LINK Search} via the Plex /hubs/search API.

  - Native MP3 and FLAC playback. See @{"FLAC direct stream" LINK FLAC}.

  - Progressive @{"streaming" LINK Streaming} for both formats.

  - Library @{"cache" LINK Cache} for fast startup.

  - Window state persistence (position, size, compact mode).

  - Album queue with auto-advance.

  - Pause/resume, volume slider, previous/next, progress bar with
    elapsed and total time.

  - Compact and full UI modes: hide the library browser to keep just
    the now-playing transport visible.

  - Two @{"audio backends" LINK Backends}: standard AHI or direct SAGA HQ.

  - Status line in the bottom of the window for feedback.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Requirements "Requirements"
@TITLE "Requirements"


Operating system

  AmigaOS 3.0 or newer (Kickstart 39+).


CPU and memory

  - 68030 minimum. MP3 plays without issue on 68030 50 MHz; FLAC
    decoding is heavier and starts to be comfortable from 68040.
  - 68060 and Apollo Vampire (68080) recommended.
  - 8 MB Fast RAM minimum.


Required libraries and devices

  - MUI 3.8 or newer (mui.library and the standard MUI gadgets).
  - mpega.library by Stephane Tavenard, for MP3 decoding.
  - AmiSSL 5.1 or newer, for HTTPS to plex.tv and to your Plex
    server (when remote).
  - bsdsocket.library v4 (Roadshow, AmiTCP-Genesis, MiamiDX, etc.).
  - icon.library, intuition.library, graphics.library, dos.library,
    utility.library, datatypes.library (all standard).


Optional libraries

  - cybergraphics.library, for 24-bit cover art on RTG.
  - ahi.device for the @{"AHI backend" LINK Backends}.
  - codesets.library is NOT required: international characters are
    handled internally.


Hardware

  - Network connection to your Plex server (wired or wireless).
  - Audio: either an AHI-compatible setup (Paula, Prelude, Toccata,
    Vampire AHI driver) or the SAGA HQ output on a Vampire V2 or V4.
  - Real-time clock set to the correct date and time. HTTPS
    certificate validation depends on it.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Installation "Installation"
@TITLE "Installation"


Unpack the .lha archive anywhere you like. AmiPlexAmp is a single
drawer containing the AmiPlexAmp executable, this guide, the ReadMe,
and the icon.

To install:

  1. Drag the AmiPlexAmp drawer to wherever you want it on your hard
     drive (e.g. Work:Music/AmiPlexAmp).

  2. Double-click the AmiPlexAmp icon to run it. The app starts in
     5-minute demo mode by default - see @{"Registration" LINK Registration}
     for how to remove the time limit.

  3. Make sure your bsdsocket stack is online (Roadshow, AmiTCP,
     MiamiDX...).

  4. Make sure your system clock is correctly set. See
     @{"Troubleshooting" LINK Troubleshoot} if you get a "Clock not
     set" requester at launch.

There is no automated Installer script and nothing to copy to LIBS:
or DEVS:. The bundled SDK headers in sdk/ are for rebuilding from
source -- they are not needed at runtime and can be deleted if you
just want to run the binary.


First launch

The first time you launch AmiPlexAmp, you go through the
@{"plex.tv/link" LINK Setup} authentication flow.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Setup "First-time setup"
@TITLE "First-time setup"


On first launch (no auth token stored), AmiPlexAmp opens a small
window with a 4-character code and a URL:


    Visit https://plex.tv/link from any browser

    Enter the code:  ABCD


To complete the authentication:

  1. Open a web browser on any device (your phone, your PC, etc.).
  2. Go to https://plex.tv/link
  3. Log in to your Plex account.
  4. Enter the 4-character code shown by AmiPlexAmp.

Within a few seconds the PIN window closes itself and AmiPlexAmp
moves on to server and library selection. The auth token is now
stored in PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.config and you will not have to go
through the PIN flow again unless you "Deauthorize server" from
the Project menu.

After authentication, AmiPlexAmp asks plex.tv for the list of
servers associated with your account and the music libraries on
the chosen server. If you have exactly one of each, the selection
is silent. If you have several, see @{"Servers and libraries" LINK Servers}.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Servers "Servers and libraries"
@TITLE "Servers and libraries"


Plex accounts can have several Media Servers attached to them, and
each server can have several music libraries (Plex calls these
"sections"). AmiPlexAmp lets you pick which combination to use.


Server selection

After authentication, AmiPlexAmp queries plex.tv for all the
servers associated with your account. If only one owned server is
reachable, AmiPlexAmp adopts it silently. If several are reachable,
a picker opens listing each server's name and its host:port. Pick
one to use it; cancel to abort the launch.

The selection is keyed against the server's stable Plex
clientIdentifier rather than its IP address, so it survives the
server being moved to a new network, a different DHCP lease, or
behind a different relay.

Picking later: Project / Select server in the menu re-opens the
picker at any time. Selecting a different server invalidates the
local library cache (it would describe the previous server's
content) and prompts you to re-pick the music library on the new
server.


Library selection

The Plex sections of type "artist" are the music libraries. The
same logic as for servers applies: single library is selected
silently, multiple libraries pop a picker showing each library
name as you've configured it on the server.

Picking later: Project / Select library in the menu re-opens the
picker on the current server. Selecting a different library
invalidates the cache and reloads the artist list from the new
section.


Where the choice lives

Server and library choices persist between sessions in
PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.config under the keys ServerClientID,
Server, Port, ServerName, SectionID and SectionName. The simplest
way to start fresh is Project / Deauthorize server, which clears
everything and brings you back to plex.tv/link.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Browsing "Browsing the library"
@TITLE "Browsing the library"


The main window has four list columns side by side:

  A-Z         Alphabetical filter. Click a letter to restrict the
              artists column to artists whose name starts with that
              letter. "All" shows every artist, "#" matches artists
              whose name starts with a non-letter.

  Artists     One row per artist in your music library, sorted
              alphabetically.

  Albums      Albums for the artist selected in the previous column,
              sorted by year (newest first).

  Tracks      Tracks for the album selected in the previous column,
              in their original track order.

Selecting an artist populates the albums list. Selecting an album
populates the tracks list. Double-click a track to start playback.

The cover art for the current track is displayed at the top of the
window along with the artist, album and track titles.

At the bottom of the window:

  - Search bar (see @{"Search" LINK Search}).
  - Status line showing the current state.

Across the middle:

  - Transport buttons: Previous, Play/Pause, Stop, Next.
  - Volume slider (0-100).
  - Compact-mode toggle (top-left triangle): hides the four lists
    to leave only the cover and transport visible.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Playback "Playback"
@TITLE "Playback"


Double-click a track in the Tracks column (or hit Play after
selecting one). AmiPlexAmp queues all subsequent tracks from the
same album so playback auto-advances to the next track. To start
from track #1 of the album, select the album then hit Play.

During playback:

  - The progress bar at the top shows elapsed and total time.
  - The Play button becomes Pause; click to toggle.
  - Stop interrupts the current track and resets the queue.
  - Previous jumps back to the start of the current track if it's
    been playing more than 3 seconds, or to the previous track
    otherwise.
  - Next jumps to the next track in the queue.
  - The volume slider is live: drag to adjust on the fly.

The codec format and audio metadata (sample rate, bit depth) are
displayed in the top-right of the cover area, e.g. "FLAC 44.1kHz
16bit" or "MP3 320kbps".


Behavior on track end

When a track finishes, AmiPlexAmp automatically advances to the next
track in the queue. When the last track of the queue finishes, the
status line shows "Playback finished." and the transport returns to
its idle state.


Behavior on Stop

Stop both interrupts playback and clears the queue. To resume, you
have to start a new track yourself.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Search "Search"
@TITLE "Search"


Type a search query in the search bar at the bottom of the window
and hit Enter or click Go. AmiPlexAmp issues a Plex /hubs/search
request and the three list columns are populated with the matching
artists, albums and tracks across your entire library.

In search mode, album rows are shown with their parent artist for
context (e.g. "The Wall (1979) - Pink Floyd") and track rows show
the artist and album (e.g. "Wish You Were Here - Wish You Were Here -
Pink Floyd").

Click the "x" button next to Go to clear the search and return to
the normal alphabetical browse.

Search matches Plex's own logic (case-insensitive, substring on
artist / album / track names) and respects the credentials of your
account -- if your library has artists shared from a friend's Plex
server, those are also searched.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Streaming "Streaming modes"
@TITLE "Streaming modes"


AmiPlexAmp offers two playback modes, selectable from
Settings -> Playback mode:


Stream (default)

  Playback starts as soon as ~200 KB are buffered to the local
  temporary file (typically 1-2 seconds at usable bandwidth). The
  rest of the track keeps downloading in the background while the
  decoder reads from the growing file.

  Pros: instant playback. The user perceives no delay.

  Cons: a network stall during playback can cause a glitch. For
  fast-moving network conditions (Wi-Fi roaming, sleeping a remote
  server), Download mode is more robust.

  Both MP3 and FLAC support progressive streaming. For FLAC the
  decoder uses dr_flac's custom I/O callbacks to wait when the
  decoder catches up to the network writer.


Download

  AmiPlexAmp fetches the full file to T:plexplayer.{mp3,flac} before
  starting playback. The time-to-first-sound is the time required
  to download the entire track.

  Pros: robust to network stalls. The whole file is on disk before
  decoding starts.

  Cons: 30-second wait on slow connections / large FLAC files.


The choice persists to PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.config.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Backends "Audio backends"
@TITLE "Audio backends"


AHI backend (default)

Uses ahi.device CMD_WRITE with ahir_Link chaining, giving gapless
double-buffering through the standard AHI mixer. A dedicated
producer task decodes frames asynchronously into a queue while the
decoder process consumes them and submits the IORequest pair to
ahi.device. Output quality depends on the AHI mode configured in
Prefs/AHI.

Compatible with any audio hardware that has an AHI driver (Paula,
Toccata, Prelude, Vampire AHI driver, etc.).


Arne backend (Vampire only)

Specific to Apollo Vampire (SAGA core). Writes directly to the
SAGA HQ channel 3 registers ($DFF430...) for 16-bit stereo, with a
double-buffer ping-pong pattern driven by the AUD3 hardware
interrupt vector. SAGA HQ channels 0-3 share the standard AUD0-3
IRQ vectors with classic Paula, which lets the decoder process
sleep on a Signal until the chip flips buffers -- no polling,
sample-accurate.

The result is the same pristine 16-bit DMA you get from native
SAGA tools like VaMP3, with no mixer in the path. Recommended for
the best audio quality on Vampire V2/V4 hardware.

Decoding is synchronous in the Arne path: the codec is called
between buffer flips inside the decoder process.


Selecting the backend

Settings -> Audio output -> AHI or Arne. The choice is saved to
PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.config and takes effect at the next launch. The
backend is not hot-swappable mid-session because the I/O setup
differs at startup time.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE FLAC "FLAC direct stream"
@TITLE "FLAC direct stream"


When the source track on the Plex server is a FLAC file, AmiPlexAmp
fetches the original file untouched (DirectStream) and decodes it
locally with dr_flac. This is bit-perfect lossless playback.

dr_flac is a single-header public-domain FLAC decoder by David
Reid (github.com/mackron/dr_libs). The library is compiled into
AmiPlexAmp at build time with DR_FLAC_NO_OGG, DR_FLAC_NO_CRC and
DR_FLAC_NO_WCHAR to keep the binary lean.

Progressive streaming for FLAC is implemented via custom I/O
callbacks (read / seek / tell) handed to drflac_open(). The
callbacks track a BPTR file handle that is re-opened on EOF if
the writer has appended more bytes since our last view, which is
how the decoder can read past the cached file size that
AmigaOS DOS reports.


Force MP3 toggle

If you don't have the CPU budget for FLAC decoding (e.g. on
68030 with high sample-rate sources), enable Settings -> Force
MP3 to route FLAC sources through Plex's MP3 transcoder. The
transcoded MP3 is then decoded by mpega.library. Bandwidth is
reduced and CPU load is lower at the cost of audio quality.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Cache "Library cache"
@TITLE "Library cache"


To make startup fast, AmiPlexAmp caches the list of artists, albums
and tracks of your library to PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.cache. A
1000-artist library that takes 20 seconds to fetch over the network
loads in under a second from cache.


How it works

After a successful first fetch, the entire list is serialized to
PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.cache. On subsequent launches, the cache file is
checked against the current server identity (hostname). If they
match, the cache is loaded from disk and the browse window opens
immediately. If they differ, the cache is invalidated and a fresh
fetch is performed.


Cache file format

The cache file is a binary AmigaOS-native format with a magic
number ('APAC'), a version number and a header. It is not designed
to be edited by hand. Delete it to force a full re-fetch on next
launch.


Invalidation

The cache version is bumped whenever the storage format changes
(e.g. when new fields are added). Older caches are automatically
rejected and replaced on next launch.

Manual invalidation: use Project -> Refresh to force a re-fetch
of the library, or Project -> Deauthorize server to clear the
cache along with the auth token (you will have to re-authenticate
via @{"plex.tv/link" LINK Setup} after this).


Settings -> Library cache toggle

Disable this if you want every launch to re-fetch from the server.
Useful during development or if your library changes often.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE ConfigFile "Configuration file"
@TITLE "Configuration file"


All persistent settings are stored in PROGDIR:AmiPlexAmp.config,
a plain text file with one key=value per line. It is created on
first launch and updated whenever a setting changes.


Format

    Server=192.168.1.100
    Port=32400
    ServerName=My Plex Server
    ServerClientID=abc123def456...
    SectionID=3
    SectionName=Music
    Token=Xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
    ClientID=Yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy
    Mode=stream
    UseCache=1
    AudioBackend=ahi
    ForceMP3=0
    MinimizeOnPlay=0
    WinX=72
    WinY=24
    WinW=820
    WinH=540
    FullW=820
    FullH=540
    BrowseHidden=0
    Volume=75


Keys

  Server / Port / ServerName
                  Currently-selected Plex server's address and
                  display name. Refreshed at every launch from
                  plex.tv to track network/IP changes.
  ServerClientID  Stable identifier of the selected server. Used
                  to match the same server across launches even
                  when its IP / port have moved.
  SectionID       Numeric ID of the selected music library
                  (Plex section). 0 means "not picked yet" and
                  triggers the library picker at next startup.
  SectionName     Display name of the selected library, used in
                  the picker label and the cache header.
  Token / ClientID
                  Persistent auth credentials. Don't share these.
  Mode            "stream" or "download".
  UseCache        0 or 1.
  AudioBackend    "ahi" or "arne".
  ForceMP3        0 or 1.
  MinimizeOnPlay  0 or 1.
  WinX / WinY     Last window position.
  WinW / WinH     Last window size (current mode at quit).
  FullW / FullH   Last full-UI window size.
  BrowseHidden    1 if quit in compact mode.
  Volume          0-100.


You can edit this file by hand to change settings while AmiPlexAmp
is not running. Delete it entirely to reset to defaults and start
fresh -- you will have to re-authenticate.

The library cache is in a separate file (AmiPlexAmp.cache) and not
intended to be edited.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Chars "International characters"
@TITLE "International characters"


Plex sends accented characters in its XML responses as numeric
character entities (e.g. "&#252;" for u-umlaut, "&#233;" for
e-acute). AmiPlexAmp's XML decoder transforms these to their
matching ISO-8859-1 byte where possible:

  - Codepoints U+0080..U+00FF (Latin-1 supplement) become the
    single matching byte. Western European accented characters
    (German, French, Spanish, Italian) render natively.

  - Codepoints in Latin Extended-A (U+0100..U+017F) get
    transliterated to the closest ASCII look-alike, since the
    standard AmigaOS font lacks the glyphs. Examples:

      Polish:    a-ogonek -> a, e-ogonek -> e,
                 s-acute  -> s, c-acute  -> c,
                 l-stroke -> l, n-acute  -> n,
                 z-acute  -> z, z-dot    -> z

      French:    oe-ligature -> oe, OE -> OE

      Czech:     s-caron -> s, z-caron -> z,
                 c-caron -> c, r-caron -> r

      Common BMP punctuation also normalised:
                 smart quotes -> ' and "
                 em-dash      -> -
                 ellipsis     -> ...

So a Polish artist comes out as "Ania Dabrowska" rather than
"Ania D?browska" or worse "Ania D" (truncated). A French album
title displays "Coeur de pirate" rather than "C?ur de pirate".

This is a deliberate trade-off: rendering the actual Latin
Extended-A glyphs would require a non-standard font (ISO-8859-2
or ISO-8859-15 compatible) that most AmigaOS users don't have
installed.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Registration "Demo / Registration"
@TITLE "Demo / Registration"


Demo mode

Out of the box AmiPlexAmp runs as a 5-minute demo session: a
requester pops up at the end of the session announcing the time
limit, the audio is stopped, and the application exits cleanly
when you click OK. The window title shows "(unregistered)" so
you always know what mode the application is in. The About box
similarly shows an unregistered notice.

Everything else works in demo mode - browsing, search, playback,
caching, both audio backends - so you can fully evaluate the
application before deciding whether to register.


Registering

To remove the time limit, become a supporter of the Apollo
Vampire community at:


    https://apollo-vampire-lair.com


Supporters receive an amiplexamp.key file. Drop it either in:

  PROGDIR:amiplexamp.key  (alongside the AmiPlexAmp executable)
  S:amiplexamp.key        (system-wide, available to all instances)

PROGDIR: takes precedence when both are present. On the next
launch the title bar loses the "(unregistered)" tag, the About
box shows your registered name, and the 5-minute timer is off.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


@ENDNODE


@NODE Troubleshoot "Troubleshooting"
@TITLE "Troubleshooting"


"Clock not set" requester at startup

The HTTPS certificate validation needs a correct system date.
Open Prefs/Time, set the current date/time, save it. Then
relaunch AmiPlexAmp. If you don't have a working RTC battery,
you may need to set the time manually at every boot or use an
NTP client.


"Could not open AmiSSL"

Install AmiSSL 5.1 or newer from Aminet or the official AmiSSL
repository. AmiPlexAmp links statically against the AmiSSL
inline macros provided by its SDK, so the library needs to be
present in LIBS: at runtime.


"Cannot find Plex server"

  - Make sure your Plex Media Server is reachable from the
    AmigaOS machine: try pinging its IP from a shell, or open
    the Plex web UI on another machine that's on the same LAN.
  - Make sure your bsdsocket stack is online (Roadshow,
    AmiTCP, MiamiDX).
  - If your Plex account has several servers and you picked an
    unreachable one, use Project / Select server to pick a
    different one. See @{"Servers and libraries" LINK Servers}.


Playback stutters

  - For MP3 on 68030, this is expected at higher bitrates.
    Enable Settings -> Force MP3 if you're listening to FLAC
    sources -- the lower CPU load helps.
  - On AHI, check that the AHI mode configured in Prefs/AHI is
    appropriate (44.1 kHz 16-bit stereo at reasonable mixer
    rate).
  - On a Vampire V2/V4, switch to the @{"Arne" LINK Backends}
    backend for the cleanest audio path.


Cover art doesn't appear

  - On a 256-colour AGA screen, cover art uses datatypes. Make
    sure your installation has working picture datatypes
    (jpeg.datatype, png.datatype etc.) in SYS:Classes/Datatypes.
  - On RTG (15-bit or higher), AmiPlexAmp uses
    cybergraphics.library WritePixelArray. Make sure
    cybergraphics.library is in LIBS:.


No playback starts, "Playback finished" appears immediately

This typically meant a decoder open failure in earlier versions.
v0.45+ fixes the FLAC-streaming case where the partial download
size confused dr_flac's metadata bounds check. If you still see
this, check the Plex server logs for transcoding failures.


Window title shows "(unregistered)"

The application can't find a valid amiplexamp.key file. See
@{"Demo / Registration" LINK Registration} for how to install one.


Polish / Czech artists show as ASCII look-alikes

This is by design. See @{"International characters" LINK Chars}.

Back to @{"contents" LINK Main}


